 was an early election he gets  to choose the date of the general  election this bill that we've put  forward actually fixes the date of the  general election for the 9th and the  last thing is of course there will not  be requisite time to be able to get his  withdrawal agreement bill through  there's no way that this can be rammed  through so you've got three you've got  three really important things there  tapes no deal off the table fixes  legally the date of an election and  ensures that we don't see the Prime  Minister trying to ram his withdrawal  agreement through the House of Commons  and it then  refers this issue back to the people so  we can end the gridlock in Westminster  because we've got a responsibility they  provide this not just a legislature that  functions but a government that  functions and can only really do that it  seems not to do general election  listening to what you say it feels as if  the real aim of this move to support an  election is a to try and stop wrecks  like happening or scare the potentially  and secondly to try and make sure that  the Prime Minister's brexit deal has no  chance of getting through so why on  earth where the government support it  well the government claims it wants an  election that is the stipulated  stipulated aim of the Prime Minister net  and then if a deal doesn't go through  then they want an election that's a  critical difference but they're not  going to be able to get the deal through  it must the same read in that kind of  timeframe that you and I know and you're  a seasoned observer of all of this that  there are many stages of a bill and  there are many amendments that could be  tabled to this one thing by the way I  want to be absolutely clear about this  bill the principle that we have used in  putting it together is to mess as little  as possible with the fixed terms upon a  fixed term Parliament act and that means  that it is being drafted very tightly so  there has been this issue raised about  well would you seek to change the  franchise through this bill through its  passage say for example to add 16 and 17  year olds the register or EU citizens  now we've got no objection to that those  two things in principle but it would be  at this outside the scope of this bill  and we don't have a luxury of time to  give a requisite scrutiny to those  things now the only way that this can  achieve the ambitions that you want is  if a general election effectively  producers Jeremy Corbyn Prime Minister  well I couldn't disagree more about that  what you thought would be a Lib Dem  majority government but what I am saying  is is ultimately we have to pass this  back to the people so what do you become  that you think well the outcome that we  want of course will be fighting for a  majority Liberal Democrat government and  if that is delivered we will immediately  stop this chaos and revoke the article  it doesn't however however if we don't  have the numbers in a new House of  Commons to revoke article 50 of course  will still be campaigning for a people's  vote and this is the point is we don't  really know I mean Jeremy Corbyn in  number 10 but this is the thing you're  talking 20th century politics  we're in the 21st century and if you  listen to people like John Curtis for  example widely acknowledged to be the  biggest polling guru in a country he is  saying we are in full party politics now  and no one knows what will happen at  that General and I both know that by far  the most likely scenario is either Boris  Johnson in number 10 he will not go on a  second referendum or Jeremy Corbyn who  might  so your plan defends depends on Jeremy  call in getting the keys to nominee no  it doesn't our plan depends on us  delivering as many liberal Democrat MPs  as we can at the next Parliament how  many do you think would be a realistic  proposition well I think ultimately the  public get to decide this I know what  you're trying to do trying to get me to  say either 