nt says  I mean I did think that Niki Morgan's  interview with you earlier was something  you know quite something given that in  her former guise as a Treasury select  committee chair she would have been  arguing for a very different course of  action and it was interesting because I  don't think while she you know you'd  expect the government to go this is a  gimmick astana it's not read the bill  Nicky she did not rule out the  government necessarily playing not  playing ball with this bill the thing  that she has realized she had to realize  this because she is somebody who's been  very critical of our own pride her own  Prime Minister and I think has said in  the past on the record but this is  somebody who to put it generously is  economical with the truth and the  problem that most members of parliament  have with his proposals there are many  but saying well let's have this general  election on December 12th is that there  is no guarantee in law that he will  actually follow through on that promise  once Parliament has passed a dissolution  motion it empowers the Prime Minister  under the terms of the fixed own  Parliament act to have the general a  general election netted data they're  choosing because to use the language it  is thumb by Proclamation by the Queen on  the advice of the Prime Minister and we  know that this Prime Minister is not shy  about misleading the Queen and putting  her in a compromising position and you  saw that over the poor agait ssin the  unlawful prorogation of parliament one  final question for you I know you've  been looking at something called a  Wilson Doctrine which is a convention  that MPs communications shouldn't be  intercepted by the intelligence service  what is it that you've been looking at  here well last weekend number 10 briefed  to a Sunday newspaper that they were  investigating with the suggestion that  there was intelligence gathering and  surveillance of members of Parliament's  contacts with the governments 